DevOps lifecycle: Plan → Code → Build → Test → Release → Deploy → Operate → Monitor in english
Here’s the DevOps lifecycle explained in simple English:

🔄 DevOps Lifecycle Stages
Plan → Decide what to build, set goals, and create tasks.

Code → Write the application code.

Build → Compile code, create packages or Docker images.

Test → Run automated/manual tests to check quality.

Release → Prepare the software for deployment.

Deploy → Move the software into production (live environment).

Operate → Run and manage the application in production.

Monitor → Track performance, errors, and user feedback.



Here are the key DevOps practices explained simply:

⚙️ Key Practices in DevOps
CI/CD → Automates building, testing, and deploying code.

Developers push code → pipeline builds → tests run → deployment happens automatically.

Ensures faster releases with fewer errors.

Infrastructure as Code → Manage servers and networks using code (e.g., Terraform, Ansible).

Makes infrastructure consistent, repeatable, and easy to scale.

No manual setup — everything is defined in scripts.

Monitoring → Tools watch applications and systems continuously.

Detects performance issues, errors, or downtime quickly.

Examples: Prometheus, Grafana, Azure Monitor.

Feedback loops → Continuous feedback from monitoring, users, and teams.

Helps improve code quality, system reliability, and user experience.

Short cycles mean problems are fixed faster.

📊 One-Line Summary
DevOps practices = CI/CD + Infrastructure as Code + Monitoring + Feedback loops → Faster, reliable, and continuous improvement.

